@meta
id: 06-13
title: Drill — count to the comma
chapter: 06
type: drill
skills: find-f, line-start
par: 72
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: four rows, eight clicks, one miscount
open: settlements.csv

@brief
Four settlement rows, all missing their currency field. Each needs `,GBP`
inserted immediately before the third comma — that is, directly after the
amount. Eight passes over the file.

The rep is `0`, `3f,`, `i,GBP` `Esc`, `j`.

@keys
| `0` | column one |
| `3f,` | forward to the third comma |
| `i` | insert before the cursor |

@hints
`0` before every count. The third comma from the middle of the line is not
the third comma from the start of it.
--
Do not verify each row before moving on. Run all four, then look at the file
once at the end — that is how you find out whether the count is really in
your fingers.

@solution
    four times: 0 3f, i ,GBP <Esc> j

Nine keys a row, four of them the text itself. The interesting number is the
one that does not appear: zero keys spent working out where the third comma
is.

Counted `f` is the closest thing vim has to a column selector, and it is
better than one, because it tracks the data rather than the layout. These
rows are not aligned, the amounts have different widths, and none of that
changes the rep by a single keystroke.

@start settlements.csv
ord_1042,cus_88,4999,CANCELLED,2024-03-11
ord_1043,cus_91,1250,PENDING,2024-03-12
ord_1044,cus_88,899,SETTLED,2024-03-12
ord_1045,cus_92,15750,SETTLED,2024-03-13

@expected settlements.csv
ord_1042,cus_88,4999,GBP,CANCELLED,2024-03-11
ord_1043,cus_91,1250,GBP,PENDING,2024-03-12
ord_1044,cus_88,899,GBP,SETTLED,2024-03-12
ord_1045,cus_92,15750,GBP,SETTLED,2024-03-13
